WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
it's hard to believe isn't it the tokyo  olympics are finally over it has been  a successful 17 days for team gb  65 medals in total to match the tally  won  at london 2012. only the usa china  and japan won more but as always the  tokyo games  will be remembered for far more than  simply  the medal winners joining us now to look  back at the games and some of the most  memorable moments you can see there  olympic silver and bronze medalist chris  akabusi great to see  you today i suppose you can have a lion  now have you been up  all night and in the early hours for the  past two weeks oh nick i've been i've  been absolutely  glued to it i've loved every minute of  it  and particularly it came to life for me  as an athlete you know watching the two  weeks of the olympia  athletic olympiad and some great  performances  the um let's talk about jason kenny i'm  gathering we should  do i left it to the last day many didn't  think he'd  do it but there he is getting gold and  the most decorated olympian  ever yeah i'm obviously a true olympic  performer he's got great pedigree  he's seen um sir chris hoy do his thing  he's obviously was inspired by people  like um oh gosh go but the  the role was redgrave and pin set and  he's realized  i can do this you know i could be top of  that tree so it's a brilliant accolade  for him  and great for united kingdom and talking  about uh laura as well wasn't quite to  be  today for her but hugely successful in  her own right hasn't she been and  indeed being the the flag bearer for  team gb at the closing ceremony  yeah let's say it was as a great duo  there you know that  they i remember actually when they um  got together  from the um 2012 games you know  and the surprise of the law when she  actually won  in 2012 you know that fantastic olympiad  for us  and then she's gone on with her husband  now to  set so many records between them  it's a great feat and great for them as  a household  and great for us as a nation yeah and  what else has uh  caught your eye on the track and field  high jump  final perhaps or no no no no no no no no  nick for me  uh the performance of the games  carsten valham in the formula hurdles  totally obliterated the world record and  basically  what we saw there was a roger bannister  moment  you know from for what what he has done  is rewritten the rules  or finding me as a hurdling it makes  people like myself  realize that when we were doing it with  all due respect  we were jogging you know we were we were  of our time  and we're competing under what we  thought was peak performance  but we're two seconds behind this guy  and it's just  absolutely phenomenal what he's done  you know and still hold a british record  but not for long now that people have  seen what he's done  i i predict there's gonna be a whole  host of performances now  of people running 46 and a half trying  to get into that 45 seconds  a phenomenal feat that if you're not  really linked to the front of hurdles  you quite can't  quite understand if i say edwin moses  who ran 122 consecutive finals  undefeated for 10 years double olympic  champion  would have been 10 to 12 meters  behind carsten varhom you can understand  the fate  of that man well that's a huge accolade  a huge tribute you're paying there  obviously with with your successes so  and we've had successes in sports that  we haven't had before what about the  boxing yesterday  uh gala the young brommy  yeah was absolutely brilliant and i  loved  you know seeing him and his brother  interacting  quite clearly a guy on a mission and yet  when it happened  couldn't was it couldn't actually  believe it became so understated it was  like  i've waited all my life for this i  worked so hard for this  and now and that happens doesn't it  sometimes you it's been such a dream  it's such a dream that when it comes to  reality you become stunned  very very humble in in victory there it  wasn't he  a huge success for a number of sports as  i say that that haven't been recognized  before and we've done better i suppose  this  this olympics we've done better in some  than we expected and perhaps worse than  others i'm thinking the rowing perhaps  this time not not as good  yeah look but nikki you can't you can't  always be top of the tree  sometimes at this level you're going to  accept that your competitors  are going to get that one step ahead of  you what i actually loved about this  olympics actually  was things like the skateboarding  and the bmx in things that you don't  readily  link with the olympics and we saw real  youth  virtuosity we saw gymkhana  and of course you know you saw the  essence of the games  young people answering the calming call  and of course when i talk about young  people  i've got mentioned tom daley a little  darling  you saw him with his knitting but  finally  getting that gold medal step by step  inch by inch it's plugged away and  tom is now olympic gold  medalist what is it what does it mean  for somebody  getting gold after so long  of trying so so many years so many  olympics well  i think you know whether it's gold or  whether it's like holly butch when you  get a bones medal  or law a meal when she gets a silver  what it actually does it says  all the work that you've done all the  people  that supported you the coach the  managers the asians the friends the  family  the training partner the physios  all of those people they didn't do it in  vain  but they spotted that talent they  invested in that talent  and their permission came down at the  end of the line now these guys might go  on and do some phenomenal stuff  but when you've done two three olympiads  and you've been that close  it's great to get that far and of course  we've got paris  just three years away given the fact  that these olympics  were delayed by 12 months yeah and you  know  what we saw for some people that the  delay of last year  came too late for them and yet the delay  of the last last year  gave opportunities for others again in  the athletics  you've got kelly keeley hutchison and  gemma riku  really matured in in a period when last  year they might not have been ready  now they've got another three years and  what i'm hoping for  in track and field for example we had  some tragedy  i hope that's not too strong a word kjt  kabutina johnson thompson dean asher  smith  both turned up injured and they didn't  make it this time well  i'm hoping that those three years is  close enough  for them to really get their teeth into  it and for those guys  to take on the mantle of jessica ennis  hill  greg rutherford mo fowler in athletics  we're missing  our top top leading lights and hopefully  those guys can go on and do it  in 2024 in paris yeah we'll never forget  uh super saturday from 2012 will we  chris acaba  you could go on for ages but sadly time  has beaten us thanks as ever  for coming on to the program today take  care god

